vely, maybe use the concept of T-transformations or majorization. But that might be complicated.\n\nAlternatively, think of the sum as sum_{i=1}^n (a_i / b_i ). Since the b_i are a permutation of a_i, this is equivalent to sum_{i=1}^n (a_i / a_{sigma(i)} ), where sigma is a permutation. So we need to maximize and minimize this sum over all permutations sigma.\n\nWe know the minimum is n by AM-GM, as shown earlier.\n\nFor the maximum, we need to maximize sum_{i=1}^n (a_i / a_{sigma(i)} ). To maximize this, we want to pair each a_i with the smallest possible a_{sigma(i)}. However, since sigma is a permutation, each a_j must be used exactly once in the denominator. Therefore, to maximize the sum, we need to match the largest a_i with the smallest a_j, the second largest with the second smallest, etc. This is similar to the rearrangement inequality where to maximize the sum of products, you pair the largest with the largest. But here, since we have a ratio, it's different.\n\nWait, rearrangement inequality deals with the sum of products. For ratios, perhaps we can consider that to maximize a_i / b_i, we need the largest a_i divided by the smallest b_i. But since b_i is a permutation, the smallest b_i is the smallest a_j. So pairing the largest a_i with the smallest a_j gives the maximum individual term. Then, the next largest a_i with the next smallest a_j, etc. This would indeed maximize the sum.\n\nTherefore, the maximum sum is achieved by this \"greedy\" pairing. Let's denote the sorted a_i in decreasing order: c_1 >= c_2 >= ... >= c_n. Then the permutation sigma that maps i to n - i + 1 (i.e., reversing the order) would pair c_1 with c_n, c_2 with c_{n-1}, etc. Then, the sum becomes sum_{i=1}^n (c_i / c_{n - i + 1} ). For each pair (c_i, c_{n - i + 1}), the ratio is c_i / c_{n - i + 1}. If n is even, we have n/2 such pairs; if odd, (n-1)/2 pairs and one middle term which is c_{(n+1)/2} / c_{(n+1)/2} = 1.\n\nNow, each pair contributes c_i / c_j + c_j / c_i where c_i 