_i}{b_i} \\geqslant \\left( \\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{a_i}{b_i} \\right)^{1/n} = 1 \\implies \\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac{a_i}{b_i} \\geqslant n.\n   \\]\n\n**Upper Bound (Pairwise Maximization):**\n\n1. **Sorted Sequences:** Sort \\( \\{a_i\\} \\) in decreasing order: \\( c_1 \\geqslant c_2 \\geqslant \\cdots \\geqslant c_n \\). Pair \\( c_i \\) with \\( c_{n-i+1} \\) to form \\( \\frac{c_i}{c_{n-i+1}} \\).\n2. **Pair Contribution:** Each pair \\( \\left( \\frac{c_i}{c_{n-i+1}} + \\frac{c_{n-i+1}}{c_i} \\right) \\) satisfies:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c_i}{c_j} + \\frac{c_j}{c_i} \\leqslant \\frac{q}{p} + \\frac{p}{q}, \\quad \\text{where } c_i \\leqslant q, c_j \\geqslant p.\n   \\]\n3. **Excess per Pair:** The excess over 2 for each pair is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{q}{p} + \\frac{p}{q} \\right) - 2 = \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}} \\right)^2.\n   \\]\n4. **Total Excess:** With \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor \\) such pairs:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac{a_i}{b_i} \\leqslant n + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}} \\right)^2.\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe inequalities hold with the lower bound from AM-GM and the upper bound from maximizing pairwise ratios. Thus,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n \\leqslant \\frac{a_1}{b_1} + \\frac{a_2}{b_2} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_n}{b_n} \\leqslant n + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}} \\right)^2}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the sequence of numbers \\(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\) such that \\(0 < p \\leq a_i \\leq q\\) for \\(i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n\\) and \\(b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_n\\) are a permutation of \\(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\).\n\nWe need to prove the inequality:\n\\[\nn \\leq \\frac{a_1}{b_1} + \\frac{a_2}{b_2} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_n}{b_n} \\leq n + \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2.\n\\]\n\n1. **Lower Bound Proof**:\n   - By the basic inequality, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_1}{b_1} + \\frac{a_2}{b_2} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_n}{b_n} \\geq n.\n   \\]\n   This follows from the fact that \\(\\frac{a_i}{a_i} = 1\\) for all \\(i\\), and the sum of these \\(n\\) terms gives us \\(n\\) as the lower bound.\n\n2. **Upper Bound Proof**:\n   - We assume \\(a_1 \\leq a_2 \\leq \\cdots \\leq a_n\\).\n   - By rearrangement inequality, we know:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_1}{b_1} + \\frac{a_2}{b_2} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_n}{b_n} \\leq \\frac{a_1}{a_n} + \\frac{a_2}{a_{n-1}} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_n}{a_1}.\n   \\]\n   For \\(x \\in \\left[\\frac{p}{q}, \\frac{q}{p}\\right]\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{1}{x} = \\left(\\sqrt{x} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^2 + 2 \\leq \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2 + 2.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Case 1: \\(n = 2k\\) (even number of terms)**:\n   - Consider the sum:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_1}{a_n} + \\frac{a_n}{a_1} + \\frac{a_2}{a_{n-1}} + \\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_2} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_k}{a_{k+1}} + \\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k}.\n   \\]\n   Each term pair satisfies:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_i}{a_{n-i+1}} + \\frac{a_{n-i+1}}{a_i} \\leq \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2 + 2.\n   \\]\n   Summing these \\(k\\) pairs, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^k \\left(\\frac{a_i}{a_{n-i+1}} + \\frac{a_{n-i+1}}{a_i}\\right) \\leq k\\left(\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2 + 2\\right) = n + \\frac{n}{2}\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Case 2: \\(n = 2k-1\\) (odd number of terms)**:\n   - Consider the sum:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a_1}{a_n} + \\frac{a_n}{a_1} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_{k-1}}{a_{k-1}} + \\frac{a_{k-1}}{a_{k-1}} + \\frac{a_{k}}{a_k}.\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a_{k}\\)'s self-terms add up to 1. Thus, the terms satisfy:\n   \\[\n   (k-1)\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2 + 2(k-1) + 1.\n   \\]\n   Summing these pairs along with \\(a_k\\)'s self-term (1), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{k-1} \\left(\\frac{a_i}{a_{n-i+1}} + \\frac{a_{n-i+1}}{a_i}\\right) + 1 \\leq (k-1)\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2 + 2(k-1) + 1 = n + \\frac{n-1}{2}\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2.\n   \\]\n\n   Combining both cases, we get the required upper bound. \n\n**Conclusion**:\n\\[\n\\boxed{n \\leq \\frac{a_1}{b_1} + \\frac{a_2}{b_2} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_n}{b_n} \\leq n + \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{q}{p}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{p}{q}}\\right)^2.}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The triangle ABC has an obtuse angle at B, and angle A is less than angle C. The external angle bisector at A meets the line BC at D, and the external angle bisector at B meets the line AC at E. Also, BA = AD = BE. Find angle A.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this geometry problem. It seems a bit complicated, but I'll take it step by step. So, the triangle ABC is obtuse at B, and angle A is less than angle C. The external angle bisector at A meets BC at D, and the external angle bisector at B meets AC at E. Also, BA = AD = BE. We need to find angle A.\n\nFirst, let me sketch the triangle. Since it's obtuse at B, angle B is greater than 90 degrees. Also, angle A is less than angle C, so angle A < angle C. Let me note that down.\n\nNow, the external angle bisector at A. Hmm, external angle bisector. Remember, an external angle bisector divides the supplementary angle adjacent to the angle of the triangle into two equal parts. So, for angle A, the internal angle is at A, but the external bisector would bisect the angle outside the triangle. Similarly for the external bisector at B.\n\nSo, the external angle bisector at A meets BC at D. Similarly, the external angle bisector at B meets AC at E. Also, BA = AD = BE. That's interesting. So, BA is the side from B to A, AD is from A to D on BC, and BE is 