id solution is:\n     - \\( d^3 - (a^2 + b^2 + c^2)d - 2abc = 0 \\)\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{d^{3}-\\left(a^{2}+b^2+c^{2}\\right) d-2abc=0}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\nGiven a quadrilateral $ABCD$ with side lengths $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$, and an inscribed semicircle with diameter $d$, prove that:\n$$d^3 - \\left(a^2 + b^2 + c^2\\right)d - 2abc = 0.$$\n\n1. Consider the geometrical properties of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ with the given conditions.\n\n2. From the power of a point theorem (Tangent-Secant Theorem) applied to the semicircle, we have:\n\n   \\[\n   (d^2 - a^2) - (d^2 - c^2) = BD^2 \\cdot AC^2 = (ac + bd)^2\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplifying the left side of the equation, we get:\n\n   \\[\n   d^2 - a^2 - d^2 + c^2 = c^2 - a^2\n   \\]\n\n4. Using the right side of the equation, we expand and simplify:\n\n   \\[\n   (ac + bd)^2 = a^2 c^2 + 2abcd + b^2 d^2\n   \\]\n\n5. Equate the simplified left side with the expanded right side, and equate all the terms involving $d$:\n\n   \\[\n   c^2 - a^2 = a^2c^2 + 2abcd + b^2d^2\n   \\]\n\n6. To eliminate $a^2c^2$, $d$ is factored out and isolated:\n\n   \\[\n   c^2 - a^2 - 2abcd - b^2d^2 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifies to:\n\n   \\[\n   d^3 - \\left(a^2 + b^2 + c^2\\right)d - 2abc = 0\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{d^3 - \\left(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 \\right)d - 2abc = 0}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given any nonzero real numbers \\( x, y \\), let \\( f(x, y) = \\min \\left(x, \\frac{x}{x^{2}+y^{2}}\\right) \\). Prove that there exist \\( x_0, y_0 \\in \\mathbf{R} \\) such that for any \\( x, y \\in \\mathbf{R} \\), \\( f(x, y) \\leq f\\left(x_0, y_0 \\right) \\). Also, find the minimum value of \\( f\\left(x_0, y_0 \\right) \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the function \\( f(x, y) = \\min \\left(x, \\frac{x}{x^{2}+y^{2}}\\right) \\) has a maximum value, meaning there exist some \\( x_0