er.\n\ny=3:\n\nm=(15 -3)/(21 -5)=12/16=3/4. Not integer.\n\ny=4:\n\nm=(20 -3)/(28 -5)=17/23\u22480.739. Not integer.\n\ny=5:\n\nm=22/30=11/15. Not integer.\n\ny=6:\n\nm=27/37\u22480.729. Not integer.\n\nContinuing, it's clear m is less than1 for y\u22652. Thus, no solutions here.\n\nk=4:\n\n2x +3y=4(1 +x) =>2x +3y=4 +4x => -2x +3y=4 =>3y=2x +4.\n\nThus, y=(2x +4)/3. Since y must be integer, 2x +4 must be divisible by3.\n\nSo 2x +4 \u22610 mod3 =>2x \u2261-4 mod3 =>2x \u22612 mod3 =>x \u22611 mod3.\n\nThus, x=3t +1 for t\u22650. Then, y=(2*(3t +1)+4)/3=(6t +2 +4)/3=(6t +6)/3=2t +2.\n\nTherefore, x=3t +1, y=2t +2. Now, substitute into the second equation.\n\nSecond equation: x +2y = m(1 +2x +y)\n\nSubstitute x=3t +1, y=2t +2:\n\nLeft side: (3t +1) +2*(2t +2)=3t +1 +4t +4=7t +5\n\nRight side: m*(1 +2*(3t +1) + (2t +2))=m*(1 +6t +2 +2t +2)=m*(8t +5)\n\nThus:\n\n7t +5 =m*(8t +5)\n\nSolve for m:\n\nm=(7t +5)/(8t +5)\n\nSince m must be a positive integer. Let's check possible values of t.\n\nt=0:\n\nm=(0 +5)/(0 +5)=1. So m=1.\n\nThen, check if this works.\n\nt=0: x=3*0 +1=1, y=2*0 +2=2.\n\nThus, x=1, y=2.\n\nCheck if these satisfy the divisor conditions.\n\nCompute n=1 +3x +3y=1 +3*1 +3*2=1 +3 +6=10.\n\nDivisors are:\n\nd1=1,\n\nd2=1 +x=1 +1=2,\n\nd3=1 +2x +y=1 +2*1 +2=5,\n\nd4=10.\n\nWhich is n=10. This works.\n\nt=1:\n\nm=(7*1 +5)/(8*1 +5)=12/13\u22480.923. Not integer.\n\nt=2:\n\nm=(14 +5)/(16 +5)=19/21\u22480.904. Not integer.\n\nSimilarly, higher t would give m approaching 7/8 <1. Hence, only t=0 gives a solution, which is n=10.\n\nk=5:\n\n2x +3y=5(1 +x)\n\n2x +3y=5 +5x =>-3x +3y=5 =>-x + y=5/3. Not integer. Disregard.\n\nk=6:\n\n2x +3y=6(1 +x) =>2x +3y=6 +6x =>-4x +3y=6 =>3y=4x +6 => y=(4x +6)/3.\n\nThus, 4x +6 must be divisible by3. So 4x \u2261-6 mod3 =>4x \u22610 mod3 =>x\u22610 mod3.\n\nSo x=3t, t\u22651.\n\nThen y=(4*3t +6)/3=(12t +6)/3=4t +2.\n\nSubstitute into second equation:\n\nx +2y = m(1 +2x +y)\n\nx=3t, y=4t +2.\n\nLeft side:3t +2*(4t +2)=3t +8t +4=11t +4