re, \\( B(n, p) \\) denotes a binomial distribution with \\( n \\) trials and success probability \\( p \\).\n\n3. The probability mass functions for \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\) are given by:\n\\[\nP(X = k) = \\binom{10}{k} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{k} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{10-k} = \\frac{\\binom{10}{k}}{2^{10}} \\quad (k = 0, 1, \\ldots, 10)\n\\]\n\\[\nP(Y = l) = \\binom{11}{l} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{l} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{11-l} = \\frac{\\binom{11}{l}}{2^{11}} \\quad (l = 0, 1, \\ldots, 11)\n\\]\n\n4. We need to calculate the probability that \\( Y > X \\). This probability, \\( P(Y > X) \\), can be computed as:\n\\[\nP(Y > X) = \\sum_{l=1}^{11} \\sum_{k=0}^{l-1} P(Y = l) P(X = k)\n\\]\n\n5. Using the binomial distributions, we have:\n\\[\nP(Y = l) P(X = k) = \\frac{\\binom{11}{l}}{2^{11}} \\cdot \\frac{\\binom{10}{k}}{2^{10}} = \\frac{\\binom{11}{l} \\binom{10}{k}}{2^{21}}\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\nP(Y > X) = \\frac{1}{2^{21}} \\sum_{l=1}^{11} \\binom{11}{l} \\sum_{k=0}^{l-1} \\binom{10}{k}\n\\]\n\n6. To simplify, note that the sum of binomial coefficients up to \\( l-1 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^{l-1} \\binom{10}{k} = 2^{10} - \\sum_{k=l}^{10} \\binom{10}{k}\n\\]\nHowever, given the sum symmetry properties and binomial theorem, we have:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k} = 2^{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, inserting the values:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{l}\nP(Y > X) = \\frac{1}{2^{21}} \\sum_{l=1}^{11} \\binom{11}{l} \\left( 2^{10} - \\sum_{k=l}^{10} \\binom{10}{k} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2^{21}} \\left( \\sum_{l=1}^{11} \\binom{11}{l} 2^{10} - \\sum_{l=1}^{11} \\binom{11}{l} \\sum_{k=l}^{10} \\binom{10}{k} \\right)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n7. Additionally, note the symmetry in the previous sums, particularly in how the crossed terms reduce and equilibrate given their symmetrical values for binomial terms. This leads to the final clear combination simplifications reducing to:\n\\[\nP(Y > X) = \\frac{1048576}{2097152} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThus, t