led isoceles triangle with angles 90\u00b0, 45\u00b0, 45\u00b0. Therefore, angles are 90\u00b0, 45\u00b0, 45\u00b0.\n\nBut wait, let's check if this is the only possibility. Because earlier, we concluded that angle A must be 90\u00b0, but is that necessarily the case?\n\nWait, let's retrace. We had the two inequalities:\n\nsin A sin C \u2265 sin B,\n\nsin A sin B \u2265 sin C.\n\nMultiplying them gives sin\u00b2 A sin B sin C \u2265 sin B sin C, leading to sin\u00b2 A \u2265 1, hence sin A = 1, so A = 90\u00b0. Therefore, angle A must be 90\u00b0, so the triangle is right-angled at A. Then, using the conditions h_b \u2265 b and h_c \u2265 c, which in this case h_b = c and h_c = b. Therefore, c \u2265 b and b \u2265 c implies c = b, so the triangle is isoceles with legs AB = AC, hence angles at B and C are 45\u00b0 each.\n\nBut let's verify this. Suppose we have a right-angled triangle at A, with legs AB = c, AC = b, and hypotenuse BC = a. Then, as above, the altitude from B to AC is AB = c, and the altitude from C to AB is AC = b. So, the problem states that h_b \u2265 AC (since h_b is dropped onto AC, which is side b) and h_c \u2265 AB (since h_c is dropped onto AB, which is side c). Therefore:\n\nh_b = c \u2265 b,\n\nh_c = b \u2265 c,\n\nWhich implies that c = b. Hence, triangle is isoceles right-angled, angles 90\u00b0, 45\u00b0, 45\u00b0. Therefore, the angles are 90\u00b0, 45\u00b0, 45\u00b0, so the answer would be angles of 90\u00b0, 45\u00b0, and 45\u00b0.\n\nBut wait, let's check if there are other possibilities. Suppose angle A is not 90\u00b0, but somehow the inequalities still hold. But according to our earlier deduction, sin\u00b2 A \u2265 1, which requires sin A = 1, hence angle A must be 90\u00b0, so that's unique. Therefore, the triangle must be right-angled at A, and isoceles, so angles are 90\u00b0, 45\u00b0, 45\u00b0.\n\nBut let's think again. Suppose the triangle is not right-angled. Is there a triangle where h_b \u2265 b and h_c \u2265 c without 