have G_i = R_i +1. Therefore, to maximize N, we need to maximize B, but B cannot be so large that there are not enough receivers to allow the initial students to receive back the balls and give them again.\n\nWait, but if we have too many initial balls, then there might not be enough students without balls to receive them, limiting the number of transfers. For example, if B=13, then 13 students start with balls, and 14 without. Each initial student can give their ball to a non-initial student. Then, those 14 non-initial students receive balls. If they give the balls back to the initial students, then the initial students can give again. Each time this cycle happens, the initial students can increment both their R_i and G_i. So, if this cycle repeats multiple times, the initial students can have G_i = R_i +1 (since they started with a ball). Each cycle would involve the initial students giving their balls to the non-initial students, then the non-initial students giving them back.\n\nBut in this case, each time the initial students give a ball, they lose it, so to give again, they need to receive it back. So, each cycle consists of:\n\n1. Initial students give their balls to non-initial students (G_i increments by 1 for each initial student, R_j increments by 1 for each non-initial student).\n2. Non-initial students give the balls back to the initial students (G_j increments by 1 for each non-initial student, R_i increments by 1 for each initial student).\n\nEach such cycle allows the initial students to have G_i and R_i both incremented by 1, and the non-initial students have G_j and R_j both incremented by 1. Therefore, after k such cycles, each initial student has G_i = k +1 (initial give plus k cycles) and R_i = k. Each non-initial student involved in the cycles has G_j = k and R_j = k +1 (if they received a ball in the first transfer and then gave it back). Wait, no. Let's clarify:\n\nFirst, in the first transfer, initial students give to non-initial students:\n