time a non-initial student gives a ball, they must have received one first. Therefore, non-initial students can only give after receiving.\n\nAlternatively, if after the initial transfer, non-initial students give the balls back to the initial students, allowing the initial students to give again. But as we saw earlier, this results in G_i = R_i for the initial students.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach: initial students give their balls to non-initial students, and then those non-initial students give the balls to other non-initial students. In this case, the initial students have G_i=1, R_i=0. The first receivers have R_i=1, G_i=1 (if they give it away). The second receivers have R_i=1, G_i=0 (if they don't give it). So, in this case, the initial students have R_i < G_i, while the first receivers have R_i=G_i, and the second receivers have R_i > G_i. Therefore, N=B in this case.\n\nBut this is only a single transfer. To maximize N=B, we need all B initial students to have G_i=1 and R_i=0. But if we do more transfers, can the initial students have higher G_i?\n\nSuppose B=13, 13 initial students. They each give their ball to 13 non-initial students. Then, those 13 non-initial students can give their balls to another 13 non-initial students (there are 27 -13=14 non-initial, so after giving to 13, one remains). Then, those 13 non-initial students can give back to the initial students. Now, initial students receive a ball (R_i=1) and can give again (G_i=2). Then, the non-initial students who gave back have G_i=1, R_i=1. If this cycle continues, the initial students can keep receiving and giving, increasing both R_i and G_i. However, each time they receive, they can give again. So, for each receive, they can give once. Therefore, G_i = initial give + number of times they received. Since they started with G_i=1, and each receive allows them to give again. Therefore, G_i =1 + R_i. Therefore, R_i = G_i -1, so R_i < G_i. Therefore, the initial students can have G_i as lar