\nYes, in a triangle, the distance from a vertex to the orthocenter is 2R cos A. Similarly, BH = 2R cos B, and CH = 2R cos C. Also, the length of the altitude from A is given by AA1 = b c / (2R), where b and c are the lengths of the sides opposite to B and C. Wait, no. The altitude from A is equal to 2\u0394 / BC, where \u0394 is the area of the triangle. But maybe I need to relate AA1 and AA2.\n\nWait, let me think. If AA2 is the length from A along the altitude to A2 on the circumcircle, then since A is already on the circumcircle, A2 is another intersection point. So AA2 is the length of the chord from A through A1 to A2. So maybe we can use the chord length formula. If two points on a circle are separated by an angle \u03b8, then the chord length is 2R sin \u03b8. Wait, not exactly. Wait, chord length is 2R sin(\u03b8/2), where \u03b8 is the central angle. Wait, maybe not. Let's recall that chord length is 2R sin(\u03b8/2), where \u03b8 is the central angle subtended by the chord.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using coordinates. Let me place the circumcircle as the unit circle in the coordinate plane. Let me assume that the triangle is inscribed in the unit circle. Let me assign coordinates to A, B, C as points on the unit circle. Let\u2019s denote A as (1, 0) for simplicity. Wait, but if the triangle is acute, then all angles are less than 90 degrees, so the coordinates can be set accordingly. Maybe using complex numbers?\n\nAlternatively, use vector coordinates. Let me see.\n\nSuppose we have triangle ABC inscribed in the unit circle. Let\u2019s denote the coordinates of A, B, C as complex numbers on the unit circle. The altitude from A to BC: since BC is a chord of the circle, the altitude from A is the line perpendicular to BC passing through A. Wait, but in a triangle inscribed in a circle, the altitude from A to BC is not necessarily passing through the diametrically opposite point unless the triangle is right-angled. Hmm.\n\nWait, but in an acute triangle, all altitudes are inside. The altitude from A is perpendicular to BC and passes through A. Wait, no, the altitude from A is the line through A perpendicular to BC. But since A is on the circumcircle, the foot of the altitude is A1 on BC. Then extending this altitude beyond A1 will meet the circumcircle again at A2. So we need to find the length from A to A2 along this altitude.\n\nAlternatively, parametrize the altitude line and find its other intersection with the circumcircle. Let me try this.\n\nLet\u2019s consider the altitude from A to BC. Let me parametrize this line. Let\u2019s suppose that BC is a chord of the circle, and the altitude from A is perpendicular to BC. Let\u2019s denote the coordinates. Suppose the circumradius is R, but let\u2019s take R = 1 for simplicity.\n\nLet\u2019s use complex numbers. Let\u2019s place point A at 1 (on the complex plane), B at e^{i\u03b2}, and C at e^{-i\u03b3}, but this might complicate. Alternatively, use coordinates.\n\nLet me instead consider a coordinate system where the circumcircle is the unit circle centered at the origin. Let\u2019s let point A be at (1, 0). Let\u2019s denote points B and C as (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) and (cos \u03c6, -sin \u03c6) for some angles \u03b8 and \u03c6. Then BC is the chord between these two points. The altitude from A is perpendicular to BC and passes through A. So the slope of BC is (sin \u03b8 + sin \u03c6)/(cos \u03b8 - cos \u03c6). The altitude from A is perpendicular to BC, so its slope is the negative reciprocal. Then the equation of the altitude is y = m(x - 1), where m is that slope.\n\nThen find the foot of the altitude A1 on BC. Then parametrize the altitude beyond A1 to find A2. Then compute AA2 and AA1. But this seems messy. Maybe there is a better approach.\n\nAlternatively, use the fact that in the circumcircle, the power of a point with respect to the circle. Wait, but as A is on the circle, the power is zero. However, the altitude line passes through A and A2, both on t